\section{Floating point}{
The validity claim is exact in real arithmetic. In floating point it holds to
a relative accuracy of order \code{1e-14}, and \code{round_slack} (see \code{\link[=bnb_control]{bnb_control()}})
is a calibrated pad of the right order rather than a proven error envelope.
The loss comes from dynamic range: de Casteljau averages coefficients whose
maximum can be far larger than \verb{sup G} -- a mixture that gives some outcome
little mass makes \code{q(x) / p_W(x)} enormous there -- and averaging numbers
\code{R} times larger than the result costs about \code{log10(R)} digits. Measured on a
deliberately ill-conditioned case (\verb{max(coef) / sup G} about 3500), the pad
absorbed all but 4e-15 of the relative error.

The practical consequence is only that \code{bound} and an independent evaluation
of \code{G} -- \code{\link[=expect_ratio]{expect_ratio()}}'s log-space sum, say -- agree to about \code{1e-14}
relative, not exactly, and either may be the larger. Nothing statistical
turns on it: a rescaling that is off by a factor of \code{1 + 1e-14} is not a
failure mode of an e-variable. Compare the two with a relative tolerance.

Only \code{list(polytope_face, exact_engine)} has a method. \code{halfspace_face} is
deliberately left without one -- it is unbounded, so there is no simplex to
subdivide, and that is a permanent gap rather than a temporary one. Use
\code{\link[=certifiable]{certifiable()}} to test before dispatching.
}

